From pmeyer160 at comcast.net  Sun Mar  2 21:12:00 2003
From: pmeyer160 at comcast.net (Pete Meyer)
Date: Sun, 02 Mar 2003 16:12:00 -0500
Subject: Re: "factor"-type variables
References: <3E6891A0@webmail1>
Message-ID: <000901c2e100$5f5c2880$6401a8c0@Office>

Dear Hugh,

The interpretations of the factors depends upon how you have
defined contrasts.  You can find this in help under "contrasts"
and "options".  The default is
   contrasts=c("contr.helmert", "contr.poly")
but many reassign it to be
    options(contrasts=c('contr.treatment','contr.poly')),
in which case the first category of the factor is treated as the
referent and the parameter estimates tell you how each of
the subsequent categories compares to the referent.  This
is often the simplest approach if you are interested in comparing
categories in this fashion.  For example, if you have race/ethnic
categories, the parameter estimates under treatment contrasts
would compare each to the referent.

You can also find more in the online manuals.

Hope this helps.

Pete

