From Nick.Ellis at csiro.au  Mon Mar  3 00:29:07 2003
From: Nick.Ellis at csiro.au
Date: Mon, 03 Mar 2003 10:29:07 +1000
Subject: Re: Operation on array subset
Message-ID: <745A64FABDC57D4E83A71D1B613687B29689F9@apogon.bne.marine.csiro.au>

The immediate solution that occurred to me is to use apply with margin 1:2.
However, the resulting array has reordered dimensions, so you have to
reorder them using aperm (see #3). The next idea was to do an ordering
within each row/column dimension explicitly using order(), but the result
has to be ordered again, so it uses 2 calls to order() (see #2). A third
idea is to reorder the result on-the-fly and load it back to the array in
its storage order (see #1). This seems to be the fastest for the tests I
performed.
 
# 1
tst<-array(300000:1, dim=c(50,60,100))
start.time <- proc.time()
j <- order(slice.index(tst,1),slice.index(tst,2),tst)
tst[] <- aperm(structure(tst[j],dim=rev(dim(tst))),3:1)
(elapsed.time <- proc.time()-start.time)

# 2
tst<-array(300000:1, dim=c(50,60,100))
start.time <- proc.time()
j <- order(slice.index(tst,1),slice.index(tst,2),tst)
i <- order(slice.index(tst,1),slice.index(tst,2),slice.index(tst,3))
tst[i] <- tst[j]
(elapsed.time <- proc.time()-start.time)

# 3
tst<-array(300000:1, dim=c(50,60,100))
start.time <- proc.time()
tst <- aperm(apply(tst,1:2,sort),c(2,3,1))
(elapsed.time <- proc.time()-start.time)

On my machine, method 1 is the fastest
> tst<-array(720000:1, dim=c(80,90,100))
> start.time <- proc.time()
> j <- order(slice.index(tst,1),slice.index(tst,2),tst)
> tst[] <- aperm(structure(tst[j],dim=rev(dim(tst))),3:1)
> (elapsed.time <- proc.time()-start.time)
[1] 3.63 0.25 4.40 0.00 0.00
> tst<-array(720000:1, dim=c(80,90,100))
> start.time <- proc.time()
> j <- order(slice.index(tst,1),slice.index(tst,2),tst)
> i <- order(slice.index(tst,1),slice.index(tst,2),slice.index(tst,3))
> tst[i] <- tst[j]
> (elapsed.time <- proc.time()-start.time)
[1] 5.357 0.461 6.570 0.000 0.000
> tst<-array(720000:1, dim=c(80,90,100))
> start.time <- proc.time()
> tst <- aperm(apply(tst,1:2,sort),c(2,3,1))
> (elapsed.time <- proc.time()-start.time)
[1] 6.560 0.701 7.671 0.000 0.000

