Subject: Query: managing projects
Message-ID: <F12D043C8E69D211B90A0010E36079C203DA2560@mail.ilim.com>

Hi,

I wonder could I get some advice from the group on the subject of organising
and managing data and functions. I have always felt that S+ has been a
little lacking in terms of data management and never found that working with
script files, projects or chapters suited they way I worked. I prefer to
have everything available to me at the command line. 

The idea of a hierarchy of databases appeals to me but it is not
straightforward (I think) to manipulate the databases. There are certain
functions I have written that I use in many of my projects and I would have
these in a database somewhere on my search path (not in position 1). If I
want to permanently change a function in this database, I need either to
shift this database to position 1, rewrite the function and shift it back,
or else write the new function and copy it into the database. To this end I
wrote my own set of library management tools for use in S+2000 (NT). I have
included an example of two functions I use, the first to shift the positions
of databases and the other to cut objects from one database into another. My
functions are quite loosely written but have served their purpose, however I
am worried that I could risk losing valuable data and functions because I
have overlooked something. Before I migrate and tidy up these functionsfor
S+6, I would really appreciate any advice or comments from S+ users who have
come across the same issues.

Regards,
Glenn

> f.shift.database
function(old.pos, new.pos, new.name)
{
# should check that no attempt is made to remove \s\_Functions check name of
old.pos
        name <- search()[old.pos]
        name.length <- nchar(name)
        if(substring(name, name.length - 7, name.length) == "_Functio") {
                stop("Illegal to move this database")
        }
        jj.vec <- c(old.pos, new.pos)
        if(all(jj.vec > rep(0, 2) & jj.vec <= rep(length(search()), 2))) {
                if(!missing(new.name)) {
                        name <- new.name
                }
                else {
                        name <- search()[old.pos]
                }
                what <- database.object(old.pos)        #
                detach(old.pos)
                attach(what = what, pos = new.pos, name = name)
                return(0)
        }
        else {
                stop("Illegal positions")
        }
}

> f.objcut
function(names, where.new, new.names = names, where.old, overwrite)
{
        objcopy(names, where.new, new.names, where.old, overwrite)
        remove(list = names, where = where.old)
}

