From v.moreno at ico.scs.es  Thu Mar  6 12:24:41 2003
From: v.moreno at ico.scs.es (Victor Moreno)
Date: Thu, 06 Mar 2003 12:24:41 +0000
Subject: varcomp model with no residual
Message-ID: <200303061224.41146.v.moreno@ico.scs.es>

I have noticed that varcomp with models with only random effects where no 
residual is expected behaves erratically. MINQUE0 method usually resuts in 
huge negative variance estimate for the residual and a similar positive value 
for the highest order interaction. Some times signs are reversed (negative 
for the interaction and positive for residual).

REML method avoids negative variance estimates, but seems to assume that there 
is allways a residual variance. Some times the highest order interaction is 
given a zero estimate and all its variance appears in the residual, but other 
times the variance is splitted between both, in an unpredictible fraction for 
each.

SAS proc varcomp suffers from similar problems, but sometimes gives results 
without residual variance. So, I suppose this is due to numerical algorithm 
problems, but I have not seen any warning in the manuals.

Of course excluding from the model the highest order interaction solves this, 
but with complex nested models it is not always easy to know if residual is 
real or I have ommited some terms in the model.

Any references to know more about this problem would be appreciated.

-- 
