From: james.holtman at convergys.com 

I think this does what you want by computing the 'cumsum' of
shrinking

subsets of the column:

#

# this computes all the cumulative sums for a column and then

#   then gets the minimum

#

apply(x.2,2,function(x){

    #

    # create a square matrix of each column

    #

    .mat <- matrix(rep(x,length(x)),length(x))

    #

    # set the 'upper' triangle to zero; need for
cumsum

    #

    .mat[upper.tri(.mat)] <- 0

    #

    # cumsum computes the running sum of each column, with
the first

    # n-elements ignored (since they are zero)

    #

    .cum <- apply(.mat,2,cumsum)

    #

    # take the minimum ignoring the first n-elements
across the matrix

    #

    min(.cum[!upper.tri(.cum)])

})

Bert
Gunter:

> If
> the data are all positive, your query makes no sense, as adding more
> elements to the sum must increase it. So I assume that you have positive
> and negative values.
>
>
>
> cumsum() can
> help, I think.
>
>
>
> Here is a
> function that finds the min of all contiguous sums of a vector that uses
> cumsum. Everything has been vectorized except for the final apply (which
> hides an efficient looping implementation).
>
>
>
> contigsum<-function(z)
>
> {
>
>
> lenz<-length(z)
>
>
> zmat<-matrix(0, nr=lenz, nc=lenz)
>
>
> pick<-(lenz
> + 1 -row(zmat) ) >= col(zmat)
>
>
> ind<- matrix(seq(along=z),lenz,nr=lenz)
>
>
> ok<- ind[
> row(ind) >= col(ind) ]
>
> ###################################
>
>
> zmat[ pick] <- z[ ok]
>
>
> min(apply(zmat,2,cumsum))
>
> }
>
>
>
> This function
> could be used in an apply statement to replace your nested for loops for
> each row, but note  that everything above the comment separator
> needs to be done only once, so that the code should be incorporated
> directly:
>
>
>
> zmat<-matrix(0,nrow=nmonth,ncol=nmonth)
>
> pick<-(lenz
> + 1 -row(zmat) ) >= col(zmat)
>
> ind<- matrix(rep(1:nmonth,nmonth),  nr=nmonth)
>
> ok<- ind[ row(ind) >= col(ind) ]
>
> apply(e.sim,1,function(x,zmat,pick,ok) {
>
>     zmat[pick] <- x[ok]
>
>     min(apply(zmat, 2, cumsum))
>
> }, zmat=zmat, pick=pick, ok=ok)
>
>
>
> For 1000 rows and 40 month, this took under 10 seconds on my not
> particularly fast computer.
>
>
>
