From pscherer at dow.com  Wed Mar 12 17:53:32 2003
From: pscherer at dow.com (Scherer, Peter)
Date: Wed, 12 Mar 2003 12:53:32 -0500
Subject: Request for information regarding nlregb
Message-ID: <24E34B7B296AD411815300805F578D94B42E73@elnte4.nam.dow.com>

Hi,

[Using S-Plus Professional Release 3 on Win98]

I have been modeling soil degradation data (61 data sets) using a collection of 
exponential, biexponential and linear (log-log) models.  Using nls on data sets 
where the observed data were sufficiently curved the fitted models successfully 
converged without difficulty.  However, for those data sets where degradation 
is nearly linear in profile (about 12 of 61) convergence of the fitting 
routine, particularly with the biexponential models, was either difficult to 
obtain or failed utterly.  This is neither surprising nor disturbing, as 
fitting a curve through linear data is a rather silly thing to do.

After attempting to fit the various models to as many data sets as possible I 
stumbled across the nlregb function.  Without setting any constraints and with 
a single set of starting values for the parameters I was able to fit all models 
to the entire collection of 61 data sets.  I then checked to be sure the 
resulting parameter estimates provided reasonable fits to the data and indeed 
they do.  Since I have had a reasonable amount of experience with nls, as well 
as other nonlinear routines, I am puzzled as to why with nlregb "convergence" 
is obtained so easily.

The point of this note then is to ask if anyone on this list has an 
understanding of what nlregb is doing or if texts or articles exist describing 
this function.  Details on the routine in the manuals offer little insight, the 
help pages provide little detail, Profs Ripley and Venables provide a var-covar 
matrix function in MASS and a brief description and example of nlregb but it is 
not enough for me to understand how this function works, documentation in the 
underlying Fortran code (in NETLIB) is light, previous nlregb queries in the 
archives address different issues ...  In addition, the output provided from 
nlregb is less than that provided with nls.  I am requesting guidance as to 
where I can learn more about nlregb.

One particular question I have is whether or not nlregb really does converge or 
if it is as sinister as SAS PROC NLIN when, confronted with a difficult 
optimization, will silently fix a parameter and then trumpet success upon 
convergence of the simplified model.  Also, has anyone generated a 
summary.nlregb function to provide results in a form more typical of regression 
routines ?  Any information you could point me to will be most appreciated.

<A pathological example>
# example of linear decay data
daze <- seq(0,100,by=10)
conc <- 100 - .95*daze
# plot degradation profile
plot(conc,daze,type="l")
# try to fit a biexponential model using nls
nls(conc ~ c1*exp(-k1*daze)+c2*exp(-k2*daze),
start=c(c1=25,k1=.03,c2=75,k2=.01),trace=T,
control=c(minscale=.000001))
# try to fit a biexponential model using nls and SSbiexp
A1 <- 25;lrc1 <- .03;A2 <- 75; lrc2 <- .01
nls(conc ~ SSbiexp(daze,A1,lrc1,A2,lrc2),
trace=T,control=c(minscale=.000001))

# fit using nlregb
BiExp.fcn <- function(param,x,y){
        biexp.c1 <- param[1]
        biexp.k1 <- param[2]
        biexp.c2 <- param[3]
        biexp.k2 <- param[4]
        y - (biexp.c1*exp(-biexp.k1*x)+biexp.c2*exp(-biexp.k2*x))
}
nlregb(n=11,start=c(25,.03,75,.01),residuals=BiExp.fcn,x=daze,y=conc)
# plot data and fitted model
example.param <- 
nlregb(n=11,start=c(25,.03,75,.01),residuals=BiExp.fcn,x=daze,y=conc)$parameter
plot(daze,conc)
lines(daze,example.param[1]*exp(-example.param[2]*daze)+example.param[3]*exp(-example.param[4]*daze))

Note that the fitted line is curved and does in fact not fit very well.  So, 
did this model "converge" ?  Why does nlregb appear to "work" ?

Thanks for any and all pointers.  I will summarize to group if responses 
warrant.

Peter Scherer
Dow AgroSciences
Indianapolis, IN

